<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claude Auguste Court</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1793">1793</a>–<a href="/years/1880">1880</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Claude-Auguste Court was a French soldier who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> in the 1820s and 1830s. He was one of several European officers, veterans of the Napoleonic armies, employed to modernise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> army. He is particularly associated with improving the Sikh artillery.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Court was born at Saint-Cézaire-sur-Siagne, near Grasse, the son of Andre Ambroise Court, a captain in the French army, and Marguerite Diaque. He studied at the École Spéciale Militaire de Saint-Cyr and joined the 15th régiment de Ligne as a sub-lieutenant on 30 January 1813. He fought in the campaigns of 1813-1814, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, Hanau, Champaubert and Vauchamps among other engagements, and was wounded in the left leg. Dismissed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat at Waterloo, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1818 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and joined the Persian forces trained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a> by former officers of Napoleon, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-ventura">Jean-Baptiste Ventura</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Court, Ventura and Paolo Avitabile travelled on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a> put Court in charge of training artillerymen, organising batteries and setting up arsenals and magazines on European lines. As director of the foundries he worked with Sardar Lehna Singh Majithia, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> rewarded him with Rs 30,000 for the first shell cast at Lahore and Rs 5,000 for the first fuse. He drew Rs 2,500 a month besides a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a>.</p><p>His brigade joined the units raised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-allard">Jean-François Allard</a> and Ventura, called the French Legion by British intelligence, which grew to 15,000 troops. In 1834 Court, commanding two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a>, annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, and he fought at Jamrud in 1837. He became a general in 1836 and around then married a Kashmiri Muslim woman, Fezli Azam Joo. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nau-nihal-singh">Nau Nihal Singh</a> in 1840 he backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-singh">Sher Singh</a>, whose assassination in September 1843 led him to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozpur">Firozpur</a> and return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with his family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Court settled between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and Marseille, where he was buried after his death in Paris on 21 January 1880. He made the first archaeological surveys and maps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and the north-west as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, was the first to excavate and record Buddhist sites there, and collected the coins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> from 1829. His collection disappeared after his death; three albums of 627 coin rubbings, found at a sale in 1994, are now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, and many of the coins passed through Alexander Cunningham's collection.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Claude Auguste Court” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1822 onwards he recruited veterans of the Napoleonic armies to drill his troops along European lines: the Frenchman Jean-François Allard raised a cavalry corps, the Italian Jean-Baptiste Ventura trained the infantry, and the Frenchman <b>Claude Auguste Court</b> reorganised the artillery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saint-Cézaire-sur-Siagne, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.65,6.79&amp;year=1849" aria-label="Saint-Cézaire-sur-Siagne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4713 616 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Claude Auguste Court"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5163 886h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5163 886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.65,6.79&amp;year=1849">What was here in 1849? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
